In an artificial hybridisation programme the steps follow in this order:
1. **Selection of parents** (ii) with the desired characters;
2. **Emasculation** (v): removal of the anthers from the flower bud of the female parent before they dehisce;
3. **Bagging** (iii): covering the emasculated flower with a bag to prevent contamination of its stigma with unwanted pollen;
4. **Collection of pollen from the male parent** (vi) when the stigma becomes receptive;
5. **Dusting the pollen on the stigma** (iv);
6. **Re-bagging** (i) of the flower, which is then allowed to develop fruits.

So the sequence is (ii), (v), (iii), (vi), (iv), (i). So the answer is option (B) (ii), (v), (iii), (vi), (iv), (i).

Parturition (delivery of the foetus) is induced by a complex neuroendocrine mechanism, and the signal for it comes from the foetus itself:
- the signals for parturition **originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta**;
- they induce mild uterine contractions called the **foetal ejection reflex**;
- this triggers the **release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary**;
- oxytocin acts on the uterine muscle and causes **stronger uterine contractions**, which in turn stimulate further secretion of oxytocin, until the baby is expelled out of the uterus through the birth canal.

So it is the fully developed foetus that starts the chain of events that ends in its own delivery.

(a) Advantages of micropropagation (any two):
- a **large number of plants** can be produced in a **very short time**;
- plants can be multiplied where sexual reproduction is not possible (plants without seeds can be multiplied);
- every plant produced is **genetically identical** to the original plant (somaclones);
- healthy, virus-free plants can be recovered from diseased plants by culturing the meristem.

(b) Any part of a plant (an **explant**) is taken out and **grown in a test tube under sterile conditions** in a special **nutrient medium**. The medium provides a carbon source such as **sucrose**, inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids and **growth regulators like auxins and cytokinins**. Because of totipotency, each explant regenerates into whole plants, and thousands of plants are produced this way.

(c) Important food plants produced commercially by micropropagation (any two): **tomato, banana, apple**.

Figure B shows autogamy. Complete autogamy requires (any two):
- **synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity**, that is, the anthers must shed pollen when the stigma of the same flower is receptive;
- the **anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other** in the bisexual flower, so that self-pollination can occur;
- autogamy is assured where the flowers are **cleistogamous** (closed flowers that never open), as the anthers and stigma lie close together inside them.

(i)
- **A**: **spermatozoa** (sperms), with their tails in the lumen of the tubule;
- **B**: **spermatid** (the scheme also accepts spermatozoa);
- **C**: **Sertoli cell**, which provides nutrition to the germ cells;
- **D**: **spermatogonium**, the male germ cell on the inside wall of the tubule.

(ii) A primary spermatocyte (46 chromosomes) completes the first meiotic division (a reduction division), so a **secondary spermatocyte has 23 chromosomes**. The secondary spermatocyte undergoes the second meiotic division to produce spermatids, so a **spermatid also has 23 chromosomes** (haploid).

(iii)
- **Spermiogenesis**: the process by which the **spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa** (sperms).
- **Spermiation**: after spermiogenesis, the sperm heads become embedded in the Sertoli cells, and they are finally **released from the seminiferous tubules** by this process.
